{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbht1ky2PpkwGiYK2NNs", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This matches an ISO 8601 calendar date (`YYYY-MM-DD`) and checks that the month and day are in range:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\nHow it works:\n- `\\d{4}` is a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` is a month from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` is a day from 01 to 31.\n- `^` and `$` make it match the whole string, so `x2026-10-01y` won't match. Remove them if you want to find dates inside longer text, and use `\\b` at each end instead.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and `1999-12-31`. It rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-10-32` and `2026-1-1`.\n\n**What it doesn't check:** it doesn't know how many days each month has, so `2026-02-30` and `2026-04-31` will pass. Regex can't sensibly handle leap years, so if you need that, match with the regex and then parse the date with your language's date library (for example, `datetime.date.fromisoformat` in Python).\n\n**Without range checks:** if you only need the format, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7220, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7220}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cca67cdc-f8c4-495e-b563-6732f46b5e98", "uuid": "75714c65-7936-4bd9-9ba6-eb268428ed96", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T14:51:51.900Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbht1NQTBZD35iESoc8g"}
